var _markdown = "\n\nFactories generate realistic test data without tripping over the model's guarantees. They are **model-aware**: a factory respects required fields, enums, constraints, and relations, so a default `create()` produces a valid row — no N+1 of forgotten columns in your test fixtures. Factories are the engine behind seeders and the standard fixture tool for the test suites (unit, integration, API, and e2e all accept them).\n\n## Defining a Factory [#defining-a-factory]\n\n```ts title=\"src/database/factories/posts.ts\"\n// src/database/factories/posts.ts\nimport { defineFactory } from '@kwiva/data'\n\nexport const PostFactory = defineFactory(Post, (f) => ({\n  title: f.fake((fake) => fake.lorem.sentence()),\n  body: f.fake((fake) => fake.lorem.paragraphs(3)),\n  status: f.pick('draft', 'published', 'archived'),\n  author: f.relation(User),\n  comments: f.count(5, Comment),\n}), { timestamps: true })\n```\n\n| Attribute helper    | Generates                                        |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `f.fake(fn)`        | Deterministic fake values via a faker instance   |\n| `f.pick(...values)` | One value chosen randomly from the given options |\n| `f.relation(Model)` | A related model, created and linked              |\n| `f.count(n, Model)` | `n` related rows (typical for has-many)          |\n\nFields you omit from the definition are still generated for you from the model's types — a string field without a rule gets plausible text, an enum gets a valid member, a boolean gets true or false. The definition is a layer of intent on top of model-aware defaults, not a complete attribute list.\n\n## Override Precedence [#override-precedence]\n\nWhen a row is generated, three sources merge in a fixed order:\n\n```plaintext title=\"override-precedence.txt\"\nmodel defaults → factory definition → named state → call-site overrides\n```\n\n| Source                                       | Wins over                               |\n| -------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| Model defaults (types, enums, `default()`)   | The baseline                            |\n| Factory definition (`f.fake`, `f.pick`, ...) | Model defaults, for the fields it names |\n| Named state (`states.draft`)                 | The factory definition                  |\n| `create({ ... })` overrides                  | Everything                              |\n\nBecause the model's `default()` values participate, a factory never has to restate a field that already has a sane default — and a call site can always pin a specific value.\n\n## Determinism in fakes [#determinism-in-fakes]\n\n`f.fake` is seeded, so a factory run produces the same sequence of plausible values unless you opt into randomness. This matters in tests and seeds:\n\n| Goal                  | Approach                                                                                    |\n| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Reproducible fixtures | Keep the fake's default deterministic seed                                                  |\n| Varied volume data    | Let the seeder/factory vary per call (`count(n)` naturally varies sequences)                |\n| Unique business keys  | Prefer `f.pick` over collisions-prone text, or set the identifier explicitly in an override |\n\nThe contract is pragmatic: deterministic by default, variable when you want volume, explicit when a value is load-bearing.\n\n## The Runtime API [#the-runtime-api]\n\n```ts title=\"the-runtime-api.ts\"\nconst post = await Post.factory().create()                  // persisted, valid row\nconst post = await Post.factory().create({ title: 'Custom' }) // persisted with overrides\nconst posts = await Post.factory().count(10).create()       // 10 persisted rows\nconst post = await Post.factory().make()                    // unsaved instance\nawait Post.factory().save(post)                             // persist an instance\nconst draft = await Post.factory().draft().create()         // apply a named state\n```\n\n| Method              | Effect                                                                      |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `create()`          | Build and persist one (or `count(n)`) rows                                  |\n| `create(overrides)` | Merge attribute overrides into the generated record                         |\n| `make()`            | Build without persisting — useful for asserting before write                |\n| `save(instance)`    | Persist an instance produced by `make()`                                    |\n| `count(n)`          | Number of rows for the next `create()`                                      |\n| Named states        | `.draft()`, `.published()` — each declared state becomes a chainable method |\n\nOverrides win over the definition, and the definition wins over model defaults. `make()` runs the same validation logic as `create()`, so invalid overrides surface in tests before anything touches the database.\n\n## States [#states]\n\nStates are named attribute bundles that override the base definition:\n\n```ts title=\"states.ts\"\nexport const PostFactory = defineFactory(Post, (f) => ({\n  title: f.fake((fake) => fake.lorem.sentence()),\n  status: f.pick('draft', 'published', 'archived'),\n}), {\n  states: {\n    draft:     { status: 'draft' },\n    published: { status: 'published', publishedAt: 'now' },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts title=\"states-2.ts\"\nconst published = await Post.factory().published().create()\n```\n\n| Behavior    | Detail                                                             |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Merge order | `base definition → state → call-site overrides`                    |\n| Composition | Call-site overrides always win over the state                      |\n| Typed       | State names and their attributes are typechecked against the model |\n\nThe `publishedAt: 'now'` form is resolved by the framework at create time, so states stay declarative while values stay correct.\n\n## Factories and Relations [#factories-and-relations]\n\nRelation helpers keep graphs consistent:\n\n```ts title=\"factories-and-relations.ts\"\nexport const PostFactory = defineFactory(Post, (f) => ({\n  title: f.fake((fake) => fake.lorem.sentence()),\n  author: f.relation(User),\n  comments: f.count(3, Comment),\n}))\n```\n\n| Pattern                            | Result                                           |\n| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `f.relation(User)`                 | Create a fresh related row for each built parent |\n| `f.relation(User, existingUser)`   | Link the given instance, never create            |\n| `create({ author: existingUser })` | Same as above, enforced at the call site         |\n| `f.count(3, Comment)`              | Three children per parent, present in the graph  |\n\nFactories for graphs — a post with comments, an order with items — pair `f.relation` with `f.count` so the composed fixture is one call rather than a hand-written tree. This composes with the model's lazy relation system, so a factory for a deep tree only pulls in the models it actually references.\n\n## Using Factories in Tests [#using-factories-in-tests]\n\n```ts title=\"using-factories-in-tests.ts\"\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'\nimport { Post } from '@kwiva/data'\nimport { PostFactory } from '../database/factories/posts'\n\nit('lists published posts', async () => {\n  await PostFactory.count(3).create({ status: 'published' })\n  const result = await Post.query().where('status', 'published').get()\n  expect(result).toHaveLength(3)\n})\n```\n\nBecause factories clean up after themselves via the test harness, look for the fixture setup on [Testing: Integration](/docs/testing/integration-testing) and [Testing: Unit](/docs/testing/unit-testing).\n\n## What's Next [#whats-next]\n\n1. [Seeders](/docs/data/seeders) — factories inside idempotent seeders\n2. [Testing](/docs/testing) — the fixture discipline across suite types\n3. [Models](/docs/data/models) — the definitions factories respect\n4. [CLI: Generators](/docs/cli/generators) — `make:factory` and related scaffolding\n";
